    {
      "id": "Ec_qdN_WRIUZvHIgXkW4c",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 287,
      "y": 4362,
      "width": 1567.039306640625,
      "height": 3650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 307175255,
      "version": 108,
      "versionNonce": 1868444057,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1768819149287,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Video Upload (Frontend / Web Layer)\n\n    Users upload videos through web servers.\n    \n    Web servers can run Apache or Lighttpd.\n    \n    Lighttpd is preferred because it is lightweight, fast, and efficient at serving static content like videos and web pages.\n\n2. Request Handling (Application Layer)\n\n    Web servers forward requests to application servers.\n    \n    Application servers handle:\n    \n        Business logic\n        \n        Reading/writing user data\n    \n        Managing video metadata\n    \n    Web and application servers are separated (decoupled) to:\n    \n        Improve scalability\n        \n        Isolate business logic from client-facing services\n    \n    Different programming languages can be used based on performance needs\n    (e.g., C for encryption due to speed).\n\n3. Multi-Level Caching Strategy\n\n    Web servers cache frequently accessed pages.\n    \n    Application servers cache frequently requested objects (e.g., metadata).\n    \n    This reduces database load and improves response time.\n\n4. Storage Architecture (Separated by Use Case)\n\n    Multiple storage systems are used for performance and scalability:\n\n    a. Upload Storage\n\n        Temporarily stores raw uploaded videos.\n\n        Used only until videos are encoded.\n\n    b. User Data Storage\n\n        Stores user account information.\n\n        Kept separate from video data to optimize access patterns.\n\n    c. Video Metadata Storage\n\n        Stores metadata like:\n\n            Video title\n\n            Duration\n\n            Encoding info\n\n    Separation ensures faster access and better scaling.\n\n5. Database Technology Choice\n\n    MySQL is suitable when:\n    \n        Traffic is low to moderate\n    \n        Concurrent reads/writes are limited\n    \n    As scale increases:\n    \n        System can migrate to NoSQL databases for high concurrency and throughput.\n\n6. Thumbnail Storage\n\n    Thumbnails are stored in Bigtable.\n\n    Bigtable is built on Google File System (GFS) and is optimized for:\n\n        Large numbers of small files\n\n        Low-latency retrieval\n\n    Makes it ideal for image assets like thumbnails.\n\n7. Video Encoding & Distribution\n\n    Encoders perform multiple tasks:\n\n        Convert raw videos into streamable formats\n\n        Generate thumbnails\n\n        Store additional metadata in the database\n\n    Encoded videos are distributed based on popularity:\n\n        Highly popular videos → CDNs\n\n        Moderately popular videos → colocation servers\n\n8. Video Streaming (Delivery Layer)\n\n    Users stream videos from:\n    \n        CDNs\n    \n        Colocation servers\n    \n        Any nearby available site\n    \n    This ensures:\n    \n        Low latency\n    \n        High availability\n    \n        Better user experience\n\nYouTube search\n\n    Since YouTube is one of the most visited websites, a large number of users will be using the search feature. Even though we have covered a \n    building block on distributed search, we’ll provide a basic overview of how search inside the YouTube system will work.\n    \n    Each new video uploaded to YouTube will be processed for data extraction. We can use a JSON file to store extracted data, which includes the following:\n    \n        Title of the video.\n        \n        Channel name.\n        \n        Description of the video.\n        \n        The content of the video, possibly extracted from the transcripts.\n        \n        Video length.\n        \n        Categories.\n    \n    Each of the JSON files can be referred to as a document. Next, keywords will be extracted from the documents and stored in a key-value store. The key in \n    the key-value store will hold all the keywords searched by the users, while the value in the key-value store will contain the occurrence of each key, \n    its frequency, and the location of the occurrence in the different documents. When a user searches for a keyword, the videos with the most relevant \n    keywords will be returned.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Video Upload (Frontend / Web Layer)\n\n    Users upload videos through web servers.\n    \n    Web servers can run Apache or Lighttpd.\n    \n    Lighttpd is preferred because it is lightweight, fast, and efficient at serving static content like videos and web pages.\n\n2. Request Handling (Application Layer)\n\n    Web servers forward requests to application servers.\n    \n    Application servers handle:\n    \n        Business logic\n        \n        Reading/writing user data\n    \n        Managing video metadata\n    \n    Web and application servers are separated (decoupled) to:\n    \n        Improve scalability\n        \n        Isolate business logic from client-facing services\n    \n    Different programming languages can be used based on performance needs\n    (e.g., C for encryption due to speed).\n\n3. Multi-Level Caching Strategy\n\n    Web servers cache frequently accessed pages.\n    \n    Application servers cache frequently requested objects (e.g., metadata).\n    \n    This reduces database load and improves response time.\n\n4. Storage Architecture (Separated by Use Case)\n\n    Multiple storage systems are used for performance and scalability:\n\n    a. Upload Storage\n\n        Temporarily stores raw uploaded videos.\n\n        Used only until videos are encoded.\n\n    b. User Data Storage\n\n        Stores user account information.\n\n        Kept separate from video data to optimize access patterns.\n\n    c. Video Metadata Storage\n\n        Stores metadata like:\n\n            Video title\n\n            Duration\n\n            Encoding info\n\n    Separation ensures faster access and better scaling.\n\n5. Database Technology Choice\n\n    MySQL is suitable when:\n    \n        Traffic is low to moderate\n    \n        Concurrent reads/writes are limited\n    \n    As scale increases:\n    \n        System can migrate to NoSQL databases for high concurrency and throughput.\n\n6. Thumbnail Storage\n\n    Thumbnails are stored in Bigtable.\n\n    Bigtable is built on Google File System (GFS) and is optimized for:\n\n        Large numbers of small files\n\n        Low-latency retrieval\n\n    Makes it ideal for image assets like thumbnails.\n\n7. Video Encoding & Distribution\n\n    Encoders perform multiple tasks:\n\n        Convert raw videos into streamable formats\n\n        Generate thumbnails\n\n        Store additional metadata in the database\n\n    Encoded videos are distributed based on popularity:\n\n        Highly popular videos → CDNs\n\n        Moderately popular videos → colocation servers\n\n8. Video Streaming (Delivery Layer)\n\n    Users stream videos from:\n    \n        CDNs\n    \n        Colocation servers\n    \n        Any nearby available site\n    \n    This ensures:\n    \n        Low latency\n    \n        High availability\n    \n        Better user experience\n\nYouTube search\n\n    Since YouTube is one of the most visited websites, a large number of users will be using the search feature. Even though we have covered a \n    building block on distributed search, we’ll provide a basic overview of how search inside the YouTube system will work.\n    \n    Each new video uploaded to YouTube will be processed for data extraction. We can use a JSON file to store extracted data, which includes the following:\n    \n        Title of the video.\n        \n        Channel name.\n        \n        Description of the video.\n        \n        The content of the video, possibly extracted from the transcripts.\n        \n        Video length.\n        \n        Categories.\n    \n    Each of the JSON files can be referred to as a document. Next, keywords will be extracted from the documents and stored in a key-value store. The key in \n    the key-value store will hold all the keywords searched by the users, while the value in the key-value store will contain the occurrence of each key, \n    its frequency, and the location of the occurrence in the different documents. When a user searches for a keyword, the videos with the most relevant \n    keywords will be returned.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
